r exchange into the mutual fund, and whether automatic investment plans stop.\n3. **Confirm where your shares will sit.** Direct-held accounts may convert automatically at the fund's transfer agent or require you to move them to a brokerage. If you must act, initiate an ACATS or in-kind transfer early.\n4. **Expect fractional-share handling.** ETFs generally trade whole shares. Fractions are typically cashed out, which can be taxable. Confirm the treatment and whether your broker supports fractional ETF shares.\n5. **Update trading habits.** After conversion, you buy and sell on an exchange through a brokerage, using limit orders to manage bid-ask spreads. Redemption at NAV no longer applies.\n6. **Review dividends and taxes.** Reinvestment settings and distribution schedules may change.\n\nNote that a full conversion differs from adding an ETF share class, which typically leaves existing mutual fund holdings unchanged.","ttft_ms":3363,"type":"result","duration_ms":5656,"uuid":"d9eaad68-3279-4acc-83bc-c8f868e5ca7b","ttft_stream_ms":393,"time_to_request_ms":58,"first_content_frame_ms":393,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
